---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Repetition" currentPath="/plates/repetition">
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Entry</a> / <a href="/plates">Plates</a> / Repetition
  </div>

  <article class="principle-document">
    <header class="principle-header">
      <div class="principle-meta">
        <span class="principle-number">E02-P04</span>
        <span class="edition">Edition 02</span>
      </div>
      <h1>Repetition creates unity</h1>
      <p class="abstract">Patterns repeated become infrastructure. The mind learns them once, then relies on them.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="principle-content">
      <h2>Statement</h2>
      <p>Repetition is cognitive infrastructure.</p>
      <p>The user learns the system once—then it becomes invisible.</p>

      <h2>Context</h2>
      <p>
        When structural patterns repeat consistently—when buttons always look like buttons, when headings always use the same scale and weight, when spacing follows the same intervals—the mind builds a model. That model becomes automatic. The reader stops consciously parsing structure and can focus entirely on content.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is why consistency matters. Not for aesthetic reasons, but for cognitive ones. Each inconsistency forces the reader to rebuild their model. Each rebuild consumes attention. When patterns repeat predictably, attention is freed for comprehension.
      </p>

      <h2>Application</h2>
      <p>
        Repetition operates at all scales. Typography: consistent heading sizes, weights, spacing. Layout: consistent grids, margins, column widths. Interaction: consistent button styles, link appearances, form patterns. Color: consistent use of accent, neutral, semantic colors. Every repeated pattern is one less thing the reader must consciously process.
      </p>
      <p>
        In practice: A button should look identical wherever it appears. A section break should use the same visual treatment throughout. Navigation should behave the same way on every page. These are not constraints—they are gifts to the reader's cognitive load.
      </p>

      <h2>Citation</h2>
      <p class="citation-text">
        This principle may be cited as: <strong>E02-P04 (Repetition)</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .breadcrumb {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .breadcrumb a {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .breadcrumb a:hover {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .principle-document {
    max-width: 700px;
  }

  .principle-header {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .principle-meta {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .principle-header h1 {
    font-size: 2.25rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .abstract {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .principle-content h2 {
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .principle-content p {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .citation-text {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .citation-text strong {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-style: normal;
  }
</style>
